        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nShould I keep a list of related incidents in each entity's frontmatter so lookups are fast?",
          "label": "evals/vault-graphrag/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "ed6504b4fa9175790ab623bb8b500818c7bdad2d09f7155e91e99bf41e92455f",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "No. Keep the link on one side only: each incident lists its entities, and you derive the reverse lookup from that.\n\n- **Drift:** A list in the entity's frontmatter duplicates facts already stated in the incident. Every new or edited incident then has to update N entity files, and any missed update leaves a silent stale answer.\n- **Fast lookups don't need it:** Build a reverse index (entity → incidents) in one pass over the incident files, and cache it. In Obsidian, backlinks or Dataview give you the same thing. For GraphRAG, load the links as graph edges and traverse them.\n- **When to denormalize:** Only if a generated index isn't enough, for example because retrieval reads single files with no index. In that case, have a script write the list into the entity frontmatter. Mark it as generated and never edit it by hand, and rebuild it on every run or in CI."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
        "testCase": {
          "description": "backlinks",
          "vars": {
            "request": "file:///private/tmp/b5/evals/vault-graphrag/cases/backlinks.txt"
